Issue 2: Recently Relevant cards look too “card-like” ❌

Right now they:

Have strong borders

Look tappable

Feel like “items to check”

They visually compete with the stream.

This risks turning Recently Relevant into:

“Top priorities”

Which you explicitly do not want.

Fix

Make Recently Relevant feel lighter than the stream, not heavier.

Concrete tweaks:

Reduce border contrast by ~40%

Slight background blur or translucency

Smaller font size than stream items

Remove any “dot” or marker that implies status

The mental model should be:

“These are floating thoughts, not tasks”

Issue 3: URLs are too raw and dominant ❌

Right now the first thing users see is:

https://www.reddit.com/r/ClaudeAI/...


This pulls the brain into:

Technical parsing

“I should clean this”

“This looks messy”

Which invites organization urges.

Fix (without adding previews or AI)

De-emphasize the URL string itself.

Example:

Show domain + title emphasis

URL only on hover or secondary line

Truncate aggressively

Good pattern:

Spent 2000 hours coding with LLMs
reddit.com · 5 Jan


Still raw. Still honest.
But less mentally noisy.

Issue 4: “Recently Relevant” has no explanation — and that’s risky ⚠️

Silence is good.
But total opacity here may confuse first-time users.

You don’t need onboarding — but you need one line of framing.

Fix (very subtle)

Add a single line under the header:

Things that seem to matter recently.

No “why”, no logic, no tooltip.

This anchors trust without inviting control.

Micro-tweaks that would make this feel exceptional

These are optional but powerful.

1. Soften timestamps

Instead of:

5 Jan

Prefer:

Earlier this week

A few days ago

Exact time increases archival thinking.

2. Reduce vertical density slightly

More whitespace = less urgency.

Inbox apps compress space.
Memory systems breathe.

3. Remove any “selection dots” or indicators

Those yellow dots currently look like:

Status markers

Unread indicators

Even if they aren’t — users will project meaning.

If something looks like state, it creates obligation.

Final verdict (PM honest)
Is this aligned with expectations?

Yes — directionally and philosophically.

Is it category-defining yet?

Almost — but only if you fix the semantic + visual guilt leaks now.

